92186045417] [17592186500271 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500271 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e2f0ff7f-2fda-5d56-9867-4253599e5ea1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500278 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fae96d0a-2517-59d2-9013-6279cfba0795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500290 #edb/ref 1071 "A car is traveling at a constant speed on a circular road. The force $F,$ in newtons ($N$), needed to keep the car from skidding is inversely proportional with the radius $r,$ measured in meters, of the circular road. If the radius of the road is $400\\,m,$ a force of $1\\,200\\,N$ is required to prevent the car from skidding. What is the constant of variation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19727363-214e-5f0e-ae2c-203a88bbcec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500290 #edb/ref 1074 "The force $F$ is inversely proportional to the radius $r$ of the road, so we have\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{k}{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the radius is $r=400\\,\\text{m},$ the required force is $F=1200\\,\\text{N}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200 & =\\frac{k}{400} \\\\ 1\\,200\\cdot 400 & =k \\\\ 480\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=Fr,$ we have that the units of $k$ are $\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}.$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=480\\,000\\,\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500309 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500309 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500309 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500309 #edb/ref 1071 "The gravitational force $F,$ measured in kilonewtons ($kN$), that a planet exerts on an object varies inversely with the **square** of the distance $r,$ measured in megameters ($Mm$), between the object and the center of the planet. It is known that Mercury has a radius of $2.5\\,Mm$ and the gravitational force exerted on an object at Mercury's surface is $35\\,kN.$ What equation gives the relationship between $F$ and $r?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500309 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f02660d-14fa-52e0-9b74-a7e342b95b5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500309 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the gravitational force of Mercury $F$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $r,$ we must have\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $F=35\\,\\text{kN}$ when $r=2.5\\,\\text{Mm}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35 & =\\frac{k}{{2.5}^{2}} \\\\ 35 & =\\frac{k}{6.25} \\\\ 35\\cdot 6.25 & =k \\\\ 218.75 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{218.75}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500331 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500331 #edb/ref 1071 "The resistance $R,$ measured in ohms ($Ω$), of a wire is inversely proportional to the **square** of its diameter $d,$ measured in meters. A device that measures resistance registers $160Ω$ in a wire of diameter $0.01\\,m.$ What equation gives the relationship between $R$ and $d?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b917a8c-b900-5585-9155-be900a8adad8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500331 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the resistance $R$ is inversely proportional to the square of the diameter of the wire $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nR=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $R=160\\,Ω$ when $d=0.01\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}160 & =\\frac{k}{{0.01}^{2}} \\\\ 160 & =\\frac{k}{0.0001} \\\\ 160\\cdot 0.0001 & =k \\\\ 0.016 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nR=\\frac{0.016}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500337 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500337 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500337 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500337 #edb/ref 1071 "The number of sales of a new brand of cellphone varies inversely with its price, in dollars. A store sells $100$ units of this new brand of cellphone when its price is $$700.$ What is the constant of variation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500337 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3fd4767-8599-5ef5-a83e-e33c37e90d62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500337 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of sales $n$ is inversely proportional to the price $P,$ so we have\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{k}{P},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when its price is $$700,$ the store sells $100$ units. We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =\\frac{k}{700} \\\\ 100\\cdot 700 & =k \\\\ 70\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=70\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500343 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500343 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500343 #edb/ref 1071 "The frequency $f$, in hertz ($hz$), of a guitar string's vibration is inversely proportional to its length $L,$ in meters. For a guitar string of length $0.65\\,m$ the frequency is $f=280\\,\\text{hz}.$ What will be the frequency of vibration if the string's length is reduced by $9$ centimeters?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b21f180b-3aa7-59a6-b309-e783112c781d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500343 #edb/ref 1074 "The frequency $f$ is inversely proportional to the string length $L,$ so we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{k}{L},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the string length is $L=0.65\\,\\text{m},$ the frequency of the string is $f=280\\,\\text{hz}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}280 & =\\frac{k}{0.65} \\\\ 280\\cdot 0.65 & =k \\\\ 182 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{182}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the frequency when the string's length is reduced by $9\\,cm,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{182}{0.65-0.09}=\\frac{182}{0.56}=325\n$$\n\nTherefore, the frequency of the string when $L=0.56\\,cm$ is $f=325\\,hz.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500361 #edb/ref 1071 "Mark's expenditure of money $E$, in dollars, is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ also in dollars. If Mark spends $$500$ when he saves $$200,$ what will be Mark's expenditure (to the nearest dollar) when he saves $$300?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d23aaba0-389e-51c2-b2cb-f09afbfdf0ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500361 #edb/ref 1074 "Mark's expenditure of money $E$ is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ so we have\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{k}{S},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that Mark spends $$500$ when he saves $$200.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500 & =\\frac{k}{200} \\\\ 500\\cdot 200 & =k \\\\ 100\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{S}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find Mark's expenditure when he saves $$300,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{300}≈333\n$$\n\nTherefore,  Mark's expenditure when  $S=$300$ is $E=$333.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
